  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA 1030-1035 hPa surface high pressure system will remain \nanchored across the mid-Atlantic states through Friday before \nbeginning to shift off the New England coast this weekend. The \nresult is continued dry weather through much of the upcoming \nweekend. High temperatures will continue to run above normal \nwith highs each day in the 50s to lower 60s. A plume of drier \nair will build over the region tonight which should limit the \ndevelopment of lake effect clouds at night. This should increase\nradiational cooling potential along with some patchy fog,\nespecially in the river valleys. An upper level shortwave will \npass across the area on Friday but will bring nothing more than \na few extra clouds and an increase in westerly winds during the \nday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nPrecipitation chances will increase by late this weekend into\nearly next week. An area of low pressure is expected to form off\nthe Southeast coast by the end of this week and track north to\nnortheastward near or off the East Coast Sunday into Monday. In\naddition, a cold front will approach from the west. The timing \nand track of the coastal system remains a bit uncertain this far\nout but looks to make its nearest approach on Monday. The track\nof the coastal low will determine how far inland rain from this\nsystem reaches. A track along the coast will likely extend rain\nas far inland as the Capital District with a track farther off \nthe coast keeping rain across far southeastern areas or even \njust east of our area. There is a likelihood of moderate to \nlocally heavy rain where the rainband sets up. Trends will be \nmonitored over the next several days. Regardless, a few showers \nare expected along the passing cold front which may transition \nto some lake effect snow showers as colder air builds into the \nregion by Tuesday.",
